<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ismail I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1487">1487</a>–<a href="/years/1524">1524</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ismail I was the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid dynasty</a>, who took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> and was proclaimed shah in 1501. He made Twelver Shia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> the state religion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, a decision with lasting consequences. His defeat by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> at Chaldiran in 1514 set limits to his expansion, but he laid the foundations of the Safavid Empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ismail-i.jpg" alt="Ismail I" width="251" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kamāl ud-Dīn Behzād · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shah_Ismail_I_Safavid,_Behzad.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ismail was born on 17 July 1487 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardabil">Ardabil</a>, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaykh-haydar">Shaykh Haydar</a>, head of the Safavid Sufi order, and Halima Begum, a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzun-hasan">Uzun Hasan</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a>. His father was killed in 1488 in a battle at Tabasaran. In 1494 the Aq Qoyunlu captured Ardabil and killed his eldest brother, Ali Mirza, and the seven-year-old Ismail went into hiding in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilan">Gilan</a>, where scholars educated him. He came out of hiding at twelve with his followers. His rise was made possible by the Turkoman tribes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> who formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qizilbash">Qizilbash</a> movement, and a group of seven advisers, the ahl-i ikhtisas, directed the Safavid revolution.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In the summer of 1500 Ismail rallied about 7,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qizilbash">Qizilbash</a> troops at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzincan">Erzincan</a>, defeated the Shirvanshah Farrukh Yassar and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a>. After he conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a> capital, he was crowned shah in 1501. One of his first acts was to proclaim Twelver Shia Islam the official religion, which began <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>'s transformation from a mostly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> to a mostly Shia country, partly through forced conversion. In 1508 he destroyed the tombs of the Abbasid caliphs, of the Sunni Imam <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-hanifa">Abu Hanifa</a> and of the Sufi Abdul Qadir Gilani, causing sectarian tension. He also wrote poetry in Turkic and Persian under the pen name Khaṭāʾī.</p><p>His recruiting among Turkoman tribes of Eastern Anatolia brought him into conflict with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. After a pro-Safavid rebellion in 1511 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-i">Selim I</a>'s accession in 1512, Selim invaded two years later. At Chaldiran in 1514 Ismail rejected his commanders' proposal for a quick attack, saying, &quot;I am not a caravan-thief; whatever is decreed by God, will occur.&quot; The Ottomans decisively defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a>, ending Ismail's conquests.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After Chaldiran Ismail lost his aura of invincibility and fell into heavy drinking. He never again took part in a campaign and left the affairs of state to his vizier Mirza Shah Husayn, who was assassinated in 1523 by a group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qizilbash">Qizilbash</a> officers. Ismail died on 23 May 1524, aged 36, and was buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardabil">Ardabil</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tahmasp-i">Tahmasp I</a> succeeded him. The dynasty he founded ruled for over two centuries, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> was unified under native rule for the first time since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-muslim-conquests">Islamic conquest</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ismail I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Shah <b>Ismail I</b>, founder of the Safavid dynasty that overthrew the Aq Qoyunlu, was Uzun Hasan’s grandson through his daughter by the Trebizond princess Theodora Komnene.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Theodora of Trebizond, daughter of John IV, married the Turkmen ruler Uzun Hasan; their daughter was the mother of Shah <b>Ismail I</b>, founder of the Safavid dynasty of Persia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-trebizond">Empire of Trebizond · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The Safavid Empire was founded in 1501 by the fourteen-year-old <b>Ismail I</b>, head of the Safaviyya Sufi order based at Ardabil in north-western Iran.</blockquote><a href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The dynasty survived under Safavid supremacy, as Shah <b>Ismail I</b> of Safavid Iran was occupied with his wider conquests, and rulers such as Ibrahim II Shaykhshah and Khalilullah II governed as tributary princes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/shirvanshah-state">Shirvanshah State · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Qarakhanid dynasty called itself the “family of Afrasiyab”, the Shirvanshahs took many of their names from it, and Shah <b>Ismail I</b>, founder of the Safavid dynasty, named all his sons after its characters and used it as a reassertion of Persian royal authority.</blockquote><a href="/work/shahnameh">Shahnameh · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>At Chaldiran in 1514, Ottoman guns and Janissary musketeers defeated the cavalry of the Safavid shah <b>Ismail I</b>. In 1516 and 1517, at Marj Dabiq and Ridaniya, the same weapons broke the Mamluk Sultanate and brought Syria and Egypt under Ottoman rule.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-military-revolution">The Military Revolution</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardabil">Ardabil</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.25,48.3&amp;year=1511" aria-label="Ardabil on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Ismail I"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4220 4257h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4220 4257h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.25,48.3&amp;year=1511">What was here in 1511? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
